		"category": "fiction",
		"description": "The Hour of the Star is a short, haunting masterpiece narrated by Rodrigo S.M., a self-conscious writer who attempts to tell the story of Macabéa — a poor, illiterate young woman from the northeast of Brazil who has moved to Rio de Janeiro. Living in poverty and emotional isolation, Macabéa remains almost invisible to the world around her, yet her quiet existence becomes the center of profound philosophical and existential reflection. With spare, poetic prose and deep compassion, Clarice Lispector explores themes of marginalization, human dignity, the absurdity of existence, the limits of language, and the mystery of life itself. Published shortly before the author's death in 1977, this slim novella is widely regarded as one of the most important works of 20th-century Brazilian and world literature.",
